<p>Mailchimp is one of the first tools most small business owners sign up for. It has been the default email marketing platform for over a decade, and for good reason. It is easy to get started, the free tier is generous, and it does email well.</p>

<p>But here is what has changed. Mailchimp is no longer just an email tool. Over the past few years, it has added a CRM, landing pages, social posting, and basic automation. It is trying to become an all-in-one platform. The question is whether it has succeeded, or whether bolting features onto an email tool produces something different from building them into a unified platform from the start.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Mailchimp's evolution</h2>

<p>Mailchimp started as a simple, focused email marketing tool. Send newsletters, manage subscriber lists, track open rates. It was excellent at that, and for many businesses, it still is.</p>

<p>The challenge came when Mailchimp tried to expand. It added a CRM-style contact management layer. It introduced landing pages. It built basic automation workflows. Each of these features works, but they feel like additions rather than foundations. The CRM is limited compared to purpose-built CRMs. The landing page builder is functional but basic. The automations are useful for simple sequences but lack the depth needed for multi-channel workflows.</p>

<p>This is not a knock on Mailchimp. It is a natural consequence of building outward from a single feature rather than designing a platform as a whole from the beginning.</p>

<h2>What Mailchimp does well</h2>

<p>Email. That is the honest answer. Mailchimp's email builder is polished, its templates are well designed, and its deliverability is strong. If your entire marketing strategy is <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email newsletters and basic automated sequences</a>, Mailchimp handles that competently.</p>

<p>The free tier lets you send up to 500 emails per day to 500 contacts. For a brand new business just getting started with email, that is a reasonable place to begin. The analytics are clear, the A/B testing is solid, and the integration ecosystem means you can connect Mailchimp to most other tools.</p>

<h2>Where Mailchimp falls short</h2>

<p>The gaps appear when you need more than email. Most small businesses do.</p>

<p>You need a CRM that tracks the full customer journey, not just email interactions. You need a funnel builder that converts cold traffic into booked appointments. You need SMS follow-ups for the contacts who do not open emails. You need automated review requests after every completed job. You need appointment scheduling that ties directly into your pipeline.</p>

<p>Mailchimp does not offer any of those things natively. Its CRM tracks email engagement but lacks deal stages, pipelines, and the context you need to manage sales. It has no funnel builder. No SMS. No review management. No <a href="/blog/booking-systems-that-convert">booking system</a>.</p>

<p>That means you end up supplementing Mailchimp with other tools. Calendly for bookings. A separate CRM for pipeline management. ClickFunnels or Leadpages for funnels. Podium for reviews. Before you know it, you are paying for five tools instead of one, and none of them share data natively. That fragmentation is exactly the problem we unpack in <a href="/blog/real-cost-of-disconnected-tools">the real cost of disconnected tools</a>.</p>

<blockquote><p>Mailchimp is a great email tool trying to be a platform. DUSA is a platform that happens to include great email.</p></blockquote>

<h2>How DUSA compares</h2>

<p>DUSA was designed as an all-in-one platform from day one. Email marketing is one feature among many, and it is built to work alongside CRM, funnels, SMS, automations, booking, and review management. Everything shares the same data, the same contact records, and the same automation engine.</p>

<p>When a lead fills out a form on your funnel page, they enter your CRM automatically. An email sequence fires. If they do not open after two days, an SMS goes out. When they book a call, your calendar updates. After the appointment, a review request triggers. All from one platform, one login, one set of data.</p>

<p>That interconnection is the fundamental difference. It is not about whether DUSA's email builder has more templates than Mailchimp's. It is about whether your email tool knows what your CRM knows, what your calendar knows, and what your review management knows. In DUSA, it does. In Mailchimp, it does not.</p>

